[V:S] F2 | A3/2 A/ A2 G2 | F3/2 F/ F2 G2 | A c B2 A2 | (A2 G2) B2 | A3/2 A/ A2 d2 | c3/2 c/ c2 F2 | G B A2 G2 | F4 |]
w: 1.~Give thanks to God, call on his name; to men his deeds make known. _ Sing ye to him, sing psalms; pro- claim his won- drous works each one.
w: 2.~See that ye in his ho- ly name to glo- ry do ac- cord; _ And let the heart of ev'- ry one re- joice that seeks the Lord.
w: 3.~The Lord Al- might- y, and his strength, with stead- fast hearts seek ye: _ His bless- ed and his gra- cious face seek ye con- ti- nual- ly.
w: 4.~Think on the works that he hath done, which ad- mi- ra- tion breed; _ His won- ders, and the judg- ments all which from his mouth pro- ceed;
w: 5.~O ye that are of Abr'- ham's race, his ser- vant well ap- prov'n; _ And ye that Ja- cob's child- ren are, whom he chose for his own.
w: 6.~Be- cause he, and he on- ly, is the might- y Lord our God; _ And his most right- eous judg- ments are in all the earth a- broad.
w: 7.~His cov'- nant he re- mem- bered hath, that it may ev- er stand: _ To thou- sand gen- er- a- ti- ons the word he did com- mand.
w: 8.~Which cov- e- nant he firm- ly made with faith- ful A- bra- ham, _ And un- to I- saac, by his oath, he did re- new the same:
w: 9.~And un- to Ja- cob, for a law, he made it firm and sure, _ A cov- e- nant to Is- ra- el, which ev- er should en- dure.
w: * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * *
w: 11.~While yet they went from land to land with- out a sure a- bode; _ And while through sun- dry king- doms they did wan- der far a- broad;
w: 12.~Yet, no- twith- stan- ding suf- fered he no man to do them wrong: _ Yea, for their sakes, he did re- prove kings, who were great and strong.
w: 13.~Thus did he say, Touch ye not those that mine a- noint- ed be, _ Nor do the pro- phets a- ny harm that do per- tain to me.
w: 14.~He called for fa- mine on the land, he brake the staff of bread: _ But yet he sent a man be- fore, by whom they should be fed;
w: 15.~Ev'n Jo- seph, whom un- na- t'ral- ly sell for a slave did they; _ Whose feet with fet- ters they did hurt, and he in i- rons lay;
w: 16.~Un- til the time that his word came to give him lib- er- ty; _ The word and pur- pose of the Lord did him in pri- son try.
w: 17.~Then sent the king, and did com- mand that he en- larged should be: _ He that the peo- ple's rul- er was did send to set him free.
w: 18.~A lord to rule his fa- mi- ly he raised him, as most fit; _ To him of all that he po- ssessed he did the charge com- mit:
w: 19.~That he might at his plea- sure bind the prin- ces of the land; _ And he might teach his se- na- tors wis- dom to un- der- stand.
w: 20.~The peo- ple then of Is- ra- el down in- to E- gypt came; _ And Ja- cob al- so so- journ- èd with- in the land of Ham.
w: 21.~And he did great- ly by his pow'r in- crease his peo- ple there; _ And stron- ger than their e- ne- mies they by his bless- ing were.
w: 22.~Their heart he turn- èd to en- vy his folk ma- li- ciou- sly, _ With those that his own ser- vants were to deal in subt- le- ty.
w: 23.~His ser- vant Mo- ses he did send, Aa- ron his cho- sen one. _ By these his signs and won- ders great in Ham's land were made known.
w: 24.~Dark- ness he sent, and made it dark; his word they did o- bey. _ He turned their wa- ters in- to blood, and he their fish did slay.
w: 25.~The land in plen- ty brought forth frogs in cham- bers of their kings. _ His word all sorts of flies and lice in all their bor- ders brings.
w: 26.~He hail for rain, and fla- ming fire in- to their land he sent: _ And he their vines and fig\-- trees smote: trees of their coasts he rent.
w: 27.~He spake, and ca- ter- pil- lars came, lo- custs did much a- bound; _ Which in their land all herbs con- sumed, and all fruits of their ground.
w: 28.~He smote all first\-- born in their land, chief of their strength each one. _ With gold and sil- ver brought them forth, weak in their tribes were none.
w: 29.~E- gypt was glad when forth they went, their fear on them did light. _ He spread a cloud for co- ve- ring, and fire to shine by night.
w: 30.~They asked, and he brought quails: with bread of heav'n he fill- èd them. _ He o- pened rocks, floods gushed, and ran in de- serts like a stream.
w: 31.~For on his ho- ly pro- mise he, and ser- vant Ab- r'ham, thought. _ With joy his peo- ple, his e- lect with glad- ness, forth he brought.
w: 32.~And un- to them the pleas- ant lands he of the hea- then gave; _ That of the peo- ple's la- bor they in- her- i- tance might have.
w: 33.~That they his sta- tutes might ob- serve ac- cor- ding to his word; _ And that they might his laws o- bey. Give praise un- to the Lord.
